From the logs, the SR-IOV Config Daemon is working correctly, but the SR-IOV Operator Deployment is not.

What the logs show

The config daemon repeatedly detects that it needs to modify the node:

Adds kernel arguments:

intel_iommu=on

iommu=pt


Determines that a reboot is required:

drain-required: true
reboot-required: true

But then it reports:

handleDrain(): the node is still draining


This means the daemon is waiting. It will not configure the NICs until the node drain/reboot process completes.


---

More important issue

Your deployment status is:

oc get deploy -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

NAME                     READY   AVAILABLE
sriov-network-operator   0/1     0

This is not normal.

The operator itself is down, while the daemonset pods are still running.


---

First check why the operator isn't running

Run:

oc get pods -n openshift-sriov-network-operator -o wide

Then:

oc describe pod -n openshift-sriov-network-operator <operator-pod-name>

and

oc logs -n openshift-sriov-network-operator <operator-pod-name> --previous

if it is CrashLooping.


---

Also check the ClusterServiceVersion

oc get csv -n openshift-sriov-network-operator


---

Check the SriovNetworkNodeState

oc get sriovnetworknodestates -A

or

oc get sriovnetworknodestate -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

You will probably see the node stuck in InProgress.


---

Check the MachineConfigPool

Since the daemon wants to reboot the node, verify whether the MCP is waiting:

oc get mcp

and

oc describe mcp worker

(if SNO, also check master).


---

At this point

I don't think the issue is the SR-IOV policy itself. The logs indicate:

✅ Policies are being read.

✅ Intel NICs are detected.

✅ Desired configuration is generated.

✅ Kernel arguments are prepared.

✅ Reboot is requested.

❌ Node drain/reboot isn't completing.

❌ SR-IOV Operator deployment is unavailable (0/1).


The unavailable operator deployment is likely preventing reconciliation from finishing.

Please share the outputs of these four commands:

oc get pods -n openshift-sriov-network-operator -o wide

oc describe deploy sriov-network-operator -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

oc get csv -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

oc get mcp

These should identify why the operator is stuck at 0/1 and why the node remains in the InProgress state.
